course: chemistry
lesson: 16
title: Alkalis and soda, and how they cancel acids
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lick a lemon and it is sour; taste a tiny pinch of baking soda and it is bitter and soapy. Baking soda is a mild alkali, the opposite of an acid.
explain: When an acid meets an alkali, they react and cancel each other out: this is called neutralisation. If they cancel exactly, they make water and a salt, a chemist's word for a whole family of substances, not only the salt on your chips.
explain: Dad takes an antacid tablet for heartburn: the alkali in it cancels some stomach acid and the burning eases. Gardeners spread lime, an alkali, on sour soil for the same reason.
explain: Scientists measure acids and alkalis on the pH scale from 0 to 14: below 7 is acid, 7 is neutral like pure water, above 7 is alkali. Strong alkalis, like oven cleaner, can burn skin even worse than some acids, so they say "wear gloves".
fact: Baking soda (bicarbonate of soda) is a mild alkali; it tastes bitter and soapy, not sour.
fact: Alkalis react with acids and cancel them out; this is called neutralisation.
fact: Antacid tablets contain an alkali that cancels some stomach acid; medicines are taken only as an adult or doctor directs.
fact: Bacteria on teeth make acid from sugar, which damages enamel; toothpaste is slightly alkaline, and fluoride in it makes enamel tougher.
fact: Soap is slightly alkaline and feels slippery; strong alkalis like oven cleaner can burn skin badly.
fact: The rule "bee stings are acid, wasp stings are alkali" is mostly a myth; venom works mainly through proteins, and taking the sting out quickly and cold water help more than kitchen cures.
fact: An allergic reaction to a sting needs a doctor straight away.
fact: Gardeners add lime, an alkali, to make sour soil less acidic; blueberries and rhododendrons like acidic soil, so they are not limed.
fact: The pH scale runs from 0 to 14: below 7 is acid, 7 is neutral, above 7 is alkali, and the stronger the acid, the lower the number.
fact: Pure water is pH 7, lemon juice about 2, soap about 9 to 10, bleach about 12 to 13; milk is close to neutral, just below 7.
fact: Hydrangeas growing in acidic soil take in aluminium and turn blue; in alkaline soil they stay pink, so changing the soil can change the colour.
fact: Baking soda is a mild alkali and a gentle scrub that helps loosen burnt food; mixing it with vinegar makes fizz that uses both up and does little cleaning.
fact: When an acid and an alkali exactly cancel, they make water and a salt; most such salts are not for eating.
exercise: What kind of substance is baking soda? | A mild alkali | A strong acid | A metal | A kind of sugar
exercise: How does baking soda taste compared with lemon? | Bitter and soapy, not sour | Sour and sharp, just like lemon | Sweet and sugary, like icing | Salty, like a pinch of sea salt
exercise: What is neutralisation? | An acid and an alkali cancelling each other out | An acid slowly turning into a shiny metal | Two acids joining together to make a stronger acid | An alkali freezing solid into ice
exercise: How does an antacid tablet ease heartburn? | Its alkali cancels some stomach acid | It cools the stomach down with ice | It coats the heart with a soft layer | It adds more acid to digest the food
exercise: Why does sugar cause holes in teeth? | Bacteria on teeth turn sugar into acid | Sugar crystals scratch the enamel away bit by bit | Sugar is itself a strong acid that eats teeth | Sugar makes teeth too soft to chew
exercise: What does fluoride in toothpaste do? | Makes enamel tougher | Makes teeth whiter by bleaching them | Kills every germ in the body | Turns sugar into salt
exercise: Why do strong cleaners say "wear gloves"? | Strong alkalis can burn skin | They make your hands too slippery | They stain the skin bright blue | They are only harmful when warm
exercise: Is it true that bee stings are acid and wasp stings alkali? | Mostly a myth: venom works through proteins | Yes, always, so use the opposite kitchen cure on each | Yes, but only in the hot summer months | No, it is exactly the other way round
exercise: What helps most with a bee sting? | Taking the sting out fast and cold water | Pouring vinegar on it straight away to cancel it | Rubbing baking soda into it hard | Covering it thickly with toothpaste
exercise: Why do gardeners add lime to soil? | To make sour soil less acidic | To make the soil more acidic and sour | To kill all the worms | To make the soil darker
exercise: On the pH scale, what number is neutral? | 7 | 0 | 14 | 10
exercise: Which of these has the lowest pH? | Lemon juice | Pure water | Washing-up soap | Bleach
exercise: Where would milk be on the pH scale? | Close to neutral, just below 7 | Around 2, like lemon juice | Around 13, like bleach | Exactly 14, the highest number
exercise: Why are Grandma's hydrangeas blue? | Acidic soil lets them take in aluminium | They get more sunshine than the pink ones | Blue ones are watered with salty water | They are a kind that never changes
exercise: Does the fizz of baking soda and vinegar clean a burnt pan? | Little: the fizz uses both up | Yes, the fizz is the strongest cleaner | Yes, but only if the pan is cold | No, it makes the pan burn more
exercise: What do an acid and an alkali make when they cancel exactly? | Water and a salt | Sugar and some air | Oil and vinegar | Metal and gas
test: "acid and alkali make salt, so can i eat it?" What's true? | Not always: most such salts are not for eating | Yes, every salt from a reaction is plain table salt | Yes, but only if you eat it with chips | No, those salts are always gases
test: Ruby got stung by a wasp; her friend runs for vinegar "coz wasps are alkali". What's best? | Cold water, and get help if she swells up | Vinegar, because it cancels out the wasp sting | Baking soda, the opposite of vinegar | Lemon juice rubbed in hard
test: Grandma wants her pink hydrangea blue next year. What could she do? | Make the soil more acidic | Spread lots of lime around it | Water it with soapy water | Plant it in deeper shade
test: Jake's gardening dad is about to lime the blueberry bed. What should someone tell him? | Blueberries like acidic soil, so don't | Lime makes blueberries grow twice as big | Lime is fine for every plant | Blueberries need bleach, not lime
test: "oven cleaner's an alkali, not an acid, so it's safe on your hands innit". What's true? | Strong alkalis can burn worse than some acids | Yes, only acids can ever burn your skin, not alkalis | Yes, alkalis are always gentle | No, because it's secretly an acid
test: If you dipped a pH strip in a cup of pure water, what number would you expect? | 7 | 2 | 12 | 14
test: Little brother: "toothpaste is just for nice breath". What else does it do? | Helps cancel acid and toughens enamel | Turns sugar into vitamins | Makes your teeth grow back | Kills germs in your tummy
test: A game shows a wizard fighting an acid monster with a "neutral potion" of water. What would actually cancel the acid? | An alkali | More acid | Pure sugar | A metal sword
test: Which list goes from most acidic to most alkaline? | Lemon juice, water, soap, bleach | Bleach, soap, water, lemon juice | Water, lemon juice, bleach, soap | Soap, lemon juice, bleach, water
test: Dad: heartburn after a huge curry. Mum hands him an antacid. Why does it help? | Its alkali neutralises some stomach acid | It numbs the heart for an hour | It adds acid to finish the curry | It makes the curry less spicy
test: "baking soda tastes sour like lemon, right? they're both in the cupboard". What's true? | No, it tastes bitter and soapy | Yes, both are acids | Yes, but it's a bit weaker | No, baking soda has no taste at all
exam: Identify the term by which the reaction between an acid and an alkali is designated. | Neutralisation | Evaporation | Fermentation | Condensation
exam: It is given that a solution possesses a pH of 2. Select the statement most accurately characterising that solution. | It is strongly acidic | It is neutral | It is strongly alkaline | It is weakly alkaline
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the stings of bees and wasps? | The acid and alkali rule is mostly a myth, and venom acts principally through proteins | Bee stings are invariably acidic and must be treated with alkali | Wasp stings are invariably alkaline and must be treated with vinegar | All stings are cured completely by household remedies
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the flowers of a hydrangea become blue in certain gardens. | In acidic soil the plant absorbs aluminium | In alkaline soil the plant absorbs copper | The flowers reflect the colour of the sky | The plant is watered with coloured solutions
